.\"+++
.\"
.\"	FACILITY:	VBACKUP - OpenVMS BACKUP-style saveset utility for Linux
.\"
.\"	MODULE:		doc/vbackup.1
.\"
.\"	ABSTRACT:	The manual page of the VBACKUP utility.  The full
.\"			description is the help library: vbackup /HELP.
.\"
.\"	AUTHOR:		StarLet Squad and Ruslan R. Laishev (AKA: BadAss SysMan)
.\"
.\"	CREATION DATE:	 3-OCT-2026
.\"
.\"	MODIFICATION HISTORY:
.\"
.\"		 4-OCT-2026	RRL	X-04 : /DATA_FORMAT=COMPRESSED.
.\"
.\"		 3-OCT-2026	RRL	X-03 : Stage 3: ENVIRONMENT (VBACKUP_PIPELINE,
.\"					VBACKUP_PREFETCH), NOTES (the page cache).
.\"
.\"		 3-OCT-2026	RRL	X-02 : Stage 2: /RECORD, /JOURNAL, /SINCE=BACKUP,
.\"					/INCREMENTAL, the copy.
.\"
.\"		 3-OCT-2026	RRL	X-01 : Initial version.
.\"
.\"---
.TH VBACKUP 1 "3 October 2026" "VBACKUP @VBACKUP_IDENT@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vbackup \- save files into a saveset, restore, list and compare them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vbackup
.I input-spec\fR[,...]
.I output-spec
.RI [ /qualifiers ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vbackup
keeps copies of files in a
.IR saveset ,
after the BACKUP command of OpenVMS.  A saveset is one file or several
volumes; it holds the files with their owners, permissions, times,
extended attributes and ACLs, a checksum of every block and of every
file, XOR blocks that repair one bad block in a group, and a catalog
that lists it at once.
.PP
What is done follows from the two sides:
.PP
.RS
.nf
files    -> saveset      save (output: /SAVE_SET, a .bck name, or \-)
saveset  -> directory    restore
saveset  /LIST           list
saveset  [dir] /COMPARE  compare with the disk
saveset  [file] /EXTRACT=name   one file out
files    -> directory    copy, disk to disk
saveset[,...] /RECORD    rebuild the journal from the catalogs
/JOURNAL /LIST           list the journal
.fi
.RE
.PP
Wildcards are expanded by the utility: quote them.
.B *
matches any characters of a name,
.B %
and
.B ?
one,
.B ...
any number of directory levels.
.SH QUALIFIERS
.TP
.BI /BLOCK_SIZE= n
Block size, 8192 to 1048576, a multiple of 512; default 65536.
.TP
.BI /GROUP_SIZE= n
One XOR block after every n data blocks, 0 to 100; default 10.
.TP
.BI /VOLUME_SIZE= size
Cut the saveset into volumes of this size (K, M, G, T): x.bck, x.bck.002, ...
.TP
.BI /COMMENT= text
A note kept in the saveset.
.TP
.B /DATA_FORMAT=COMPRESSED
Compress the data (LZ4); data that does not shrink is stored plain.
Readers before X01-04 report compressed files as damaged.
.TP
.BI /SELECT=( pattern ,...)
.TQ
.BI /EXCLUDE=( pattern ,...)
Take, or leave out, the files whose stored name matches.
.TP
.BI /SINCE= time "\fR, \fP/BEFORE=" time
Save the files not older, or older, than the time; /MODIFIED (default),
/CREATED, /CHANGED say which time.
.TP
.BI /BY_OWNER= user
Save the files of one user only.
.TP
.B /SINCE=BACKUP
Save the files changed since their last save with /RECORD, as the journal
knows; the saveset is incremental and lists the others as present.
.TP
.B /RECORD
Record the saveset and the state of every file saved into the journal;
with savesets and no output, rebuild the journal from their catalogs.
.TP
.BI /JOURNAL [=file]
The journal: /var/lib/vbackup/vbackup.jnl for root, ~/.vbackup/vbackup.jnl
otherwise; with /LIST and no parameter, list it.
.TP
.B /INCREMENTAL
Restore a chain - full saveset first, incremental ones after it, oldest
first - deleting from every directory of the saveset what it does not list.
.TP
.B /[NO]CROSS_DEVICE
Go into other file systems; off by default.
.TP
.B /IGNORE=NOBACKUP
Save the files flagged nodump (chattr +d) too.
.TP
.B /[NO]XATTRS
Extended attributes, ACLs, capabilities, SELinux label; on by default.
.TP
.B /VERIFY
Read the new saveset back and compare it with the disk.
.TP
.B /REPLACE
Overwrite an existing saveset, file or output file.
.TP
.BI /OWNER= ORIGINAL\fR|\fPDEFAULT\fR|\fPuser
Owner of the restored files; ORIGINAL for root, DEFAULT otherwise.
.TP
.BI /LIST [=file]
List the saveset; /BRIEF (default), /FULL, /FORMAT=LS.
.TP
.BI /EXTRACT= stored-name
Write one file to the output file or to the standard output.
.TP
.B /COMPARE
Compare the saveset with the disk.
.TP
.B /LOG\fR, \fP/CONFIRM
Report every file; ask before every file.
.TP
.BI /HELP " \fR[\fPtopic\fR]"
The full description, out of the help library.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 \- all done; 1 \- done, with warnings; 2 \- something was not done.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ vbackup /home/rrl /backup/rrl.bck /VERIFY
$ vbackup /backup/rrl.bck /LIST
$ vbackup /backup/rrl.bck /tmp/restore
$ vbackup /home /mnt/usb/home.bck /SINCE=TODAY /VOLUME_SIZE=4G
$ vbackup /backup/rrl.bck /EXTRACT=rrl/notes.txt | less
$ vbackup /home full.bck /RECORD
$ vbackup /home mon.bck /SINCE=BACKUP /RECORD
$ vbackup full.bck,mon.bck /restore /INCREMENTAL
$ vbackup /home/rrl /mnt/disk2 /VERIFY
$ vbackup /home /mnt/usb/home.bck /DATA_FORMAT=COMPRESSED
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VBACKUP_PIPELINE
0 \- the saveset is written without the writer thread, for the
trouble-shooting; the saveset is the same.
.TP
.B VBACKUP_PREFETCH
The number of threads that read files ahead in a save or a copy;
8 by default, 0 \- none.
.SH NOTES
The pages of a saveset are dropped from the page cache once written
or read, and so are the pages of a file that was not in the cache
before it was saved.  Files that were in the cache stay there.  See
.BR "vbackup /HELP TROUBLESHOOTING" .
.SH FILES
.I /usr/local/share/help/vbackup.hlb
\- the help library;
.I /usr/local/share/doc/vbackup/format.md
\- the saveset format.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar (1),
.BR dar (1)
.SH AUTHOR
StarLet Squad and Ruslan R. Laishev (AKA: BadAss SysMan).
